>   http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_the_Walls_of_Eryx
> 
> where Lovecraft's hero[1] is stuck in an invisible maze on Venus.
> There's a layer of mud on the ground so he can't mark where he's been.

Interesting; I know HPL's fantasy/horror stories, but didn't know that
he has written SF as well. Having been curious I downloaded the story
text[*] and just finished reading it. Nice. And it's also inspiring for
the issue on topic. Besides the already proposed moving walls in mazes,
we could also add invisible mazes (as in HPL's story); Nethack already
supports that by the stone map element (as on the Plane of Earth), or
yet better, in its undiggable form, as e.g. in the barbarian's 'Locate'
quest level. A yet more pathological form would be an undiggable maze
of invisible wall elements that are moving (or rather relocated) over
time. Needless to say that such a feature would have to be implemented
scarcely; imagine the boredom of more than one of those levels per game.

As an option, in a dynamic maze (an ordinary or invisible one) once the
player got the Amulet, the frequency of maze changes could increase.

A variant of the idea could be that all mazes morph whenever the Amulet
has been retrieved.

Another nasty idea (but likely annoying to play) could be that on the
way up, as soon as one has stolen the Amulet, all mazes turn into such
invisible mazes. (Hmm.., on a second thought, I suppose that extreme
variation of the issue would not be a good idea.)

